  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n   The current optimization strategy relies heavily on local search methods starting from a single base solution. This may lead to local optima. The proposed approach combines multiple local search methods with a multi-resolution strategy and Bayesian optimization elements. It addresses the bottlenecks by:\n\n   - Using multiple starting points through random permutations and interpolation\n   - Gradually increasing problem resolution using interpolation\n   - Maintaining a diversity of promising solutions through active set management\n   - Balancing exploration and exploitation through adaptive search strategies\n\n   The method keeps the original optimization objective intact while introducing more informed search dynamics.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\n   - Variable Representation: Piecewise constant functions represented by threshold vectors\n   - Objective: min max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx - equivalent to min max_lag [dx * sum(h_i * (1-h_{i+lag})) for valid lag range]\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1, sum(h_i) dx = 1 (where dx=2/n_points)\n   - Formulation: The problem is non-convex with discrete variables\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   - **Initialization**\n     - Start with interpolated initial solution: h\u2080 = interpolate_to(initial_h_values, 400, target=200)\n     - Generate 3 random permutations of active set: spread_indices_rand(permutation, n_points)\n\n   - **Adaptive Search Loop**\n     For generation in range(max_generations):\n       current_time = time.time()\n       time_remaining = budget_s - current_time\n\n       # Multi-point evaluation\n       candidates = []\n       obj_vals = []\n\n       # Local search candidates (3 different methods)\n       candidate0 = local_search(h\u2080, n_points=400, budget_fraction=0.2)\n       candidate1 = fast_pair_cd(h\u2080, n_points=400, target=200, time_limit=min(time_remaining*0.2, 120))\n       candidate2 = gradient_descent_active(h\u2080, n_points=400, target=200, time_limit=min(time_remaining*0.2, 120), n_active=8)\n\n       # Evaluate and select best candidate\n       candidates.append(project_feasible(candidate0, 200))\n       candidates.append(project_feasible(candidate1, 200))\n       candidates.append(project_feasible(candidate2, 200))\n\n       for cand in candidates:\n           obj = calculate_overlap(cand)\n           obj_vals.append(obj)\n\n       best_idx = argmin(obj_vals)\n       h_best = candidates[best_idx]\n\n       # Update best solution history\n       if obj_vals[best_idx] < best_score[-1]:\n           best_sequence.append(h_best)\n           best_scores.append(obj_vals[best_idx])\n       else:\n           memory_decay *= 0.95  # Reduce weight of less promising solutions\n\n       # Progressively interpolate to higher resolution\n       if generation % 50 == 0 and time_remaining > 500:\n           new_n = 400 * (generation/20)\n           h_highres = interpolate_to(h_best, int(new_n), target=new_n/2)\n           candidates.append(project_feasible(h_highres, target=new_n/2))\n\n       # Adaptive time budgeting\n       remaining_budget = budget_s - time.time()\n       if remaining_budget < 60:\n           # Final polishing phase\n           if current_method == 'gradient':\n             time_limits = [0.5*time_remaining, time_remaining//3, time_remaining//5]\n           else:\n             time_limits = [time_remaining//5, time_remaining//3, time_limits_balance]\n\n   - **Constraint Maintenance**\n     The projection function ensures \u2211h = n/2 exactly and clips values to [0,1]. Change vector construction maintains optimality conditions after updates.\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   - **Initial Conditions**: Bootstrap from initial_h_values with multiple permutations (3 variations)\n   - **Time Distribution**: Budget allocation of 20% per search component, with dynamic recomposition based on improvement rate\n   - **Resolution Control**: Quadratic interpolation schedule: 400 \u2192 800 \u2192 1600 sample points\n   - **Active Set Management**: Uses dynamic-time support calculation based on local Hessian approximation (see gradient_descent_active)\n   - **Info thes**: Tracks historical solutions with exponential window averaging\n\n5. Computebudget and Robustness\n   - **Dominant Cost**: Resolution interpolation (O(n\u00b2)) and correlation (O(n))\n   - **Juvenile.g**: Initial phase: 150 evaluations at 400 resolution | Mid phase: 100 evaluations at 800 resolution | Final phase: 50 evaluations at 1600 resolution\n   - **Time Estimates**: 400 resolution: ~18 seconds | 800 resolution: ~72 seconds | 1600 resolution: ~324 seconds\n   - **Early Termination**: Check every 10 generations; if no improvement in 50 generations, switch to gradient-based polishing\n   - **Fallback Strategy**: Safeguard against numerical instability: if any candidate produces \u2265 NaN or \u221e in objective function, use projected random restart\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   - **feasibility_check(h)**:\n        assert(all(h >= 0) and all(h <= 1))\n        assert(np.abs(np.sum(h)*dx - 1) < 1e-5)\n\n   - **Overlap Calculation**:\n        dx=2/n_points\n        data = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n        return dx * np.max(data)\n\n   - **Final Check**:\n        confirmation = optimize_against_original(source_code='verify_overlap', method='SLSQP')\n\n   - **Output Format**:\n        final_h, final_score, final_n = polish_solution(best_sequence[-1])\n\n   - **Sanity Checks**:\n        if final_score > 0.3808: # Target bound\n            raise Exception(\"Failed to meet record threshold\")\n        if len(best_sequence) < 3:\n            raise Exception(\"Insufficient solution diversity during search\")\n        validation_candidates = [project_feasible(c, target=c.shape[0]/2) for c in best_sequence[-3:]]\n        for cand in validation_candidates:\n            score = compute_over_alt(cand)\n            assert(np.isclose(score, compute_overlap(cand), atol=1e-3))",
